<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madrasa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A madrasa is an Islamic institution of higher learning, typically teaching law, theology and related sciences. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, notably under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, founded influential madrasas to train scholars and officials and promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> continued this policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madrasa.jpg" alt="Madrasa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain; derivative work by MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ayyubid sultans patronised religious scholarship, endowed <b>madrasas</b> that consolidated Sunni learning in former Fatimid Egypt, and restored the Dome of the Rock and the al-Aqsa Mosque to Muslim worship after the reconquest of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jama Masjid in the fort of Gulbarga, built in 1367, is unusual for having its entire courtyard covered by domes, while the <b>madrasa</b> built by Mahmud Gawan at Bidar in 1472, with its tile-faced minarets, and the painted royal tombs at Ashtur reflect strong Iranian influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Contemporary historians describe great quantities of gold, jewels, captives and elephants brought back to Ghazni after each campaign, and some of this wealth was spent on a great mosque, a <b>madrasa</b> and palaces in the capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu Zakariya founded the Shamma’iyya, generally regarded as the first <b>madrasa</b> in Ifriqiya, and his successors endowed further colleges, libraries and the Zaytuna Mosque, whose teaching circles drew students from across the Maghreb.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kara-Khanids generally respected the Hanafi scholars of Bukhara and Samarqand, built mosques, caravanserais and <b>madrasas</b>, and endowed religious foundations, although relations between khans and the ulama were sometimes tense.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>madrasas</b> they built, the first in Morocco, trained the Maliki scholars who staffed their bureaucracy and were meant to undermine the marabouts and sharifian elements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
